# COUPLINGS THAT SURVIVE A FUSION, BESIDE THE CEILING THE PART'S SHARE PUTS ON THEM. One row
# per upper coupling that has a lower coupling underneath it between the same two layers,
# carrying what the upper filing claims and what the lower one can support.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ A COUPLING PROPAGATES UPWARD AND ATTENUATES. If layer A moves with layer B, and A is
#    fused into a bigger layer one level up, the dependence survives -- but weakens, because
#    the composed layer is only partly A. At most A's SHARE of the composed layer can move,
#    so the upper strength is bounded by the lower strength times that share.
#    ⛔ NOT DERIVABLE, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF CHECKING IT. A filer could put any number in
#      the upper document; the ceiling comes from two OTHER documents, which is a comparison
#      no single-document validator can make.
#
# ⛔⛔ SEVEN JOINS AND EVERY ONE NARROWS THE POPULATION -- upper coupling, the part carrying
#    its `from` layer, that part's document, the lower coupling, the part carrying the `to`
#    layer, and the two demands the share is computed from. A coverage table that reproduced
#    only the first two counted four rows where the rule examines one, which crossed a
#    threshold and reported a thin rule as well covered. That is the trap this repository is
#    written to name, firing inside its own coverage table -- and it is why nothing counts a
#    population except by composing the population.
-- pm:Coupling at two levels, related through pm:Fusion/pm:Part.
SELECT up.filing AS upper_filing, up.from_layer, up.to_layer,
       lo.filing AS lower_filing,
       lo.low  * (pd.d_low  / cd.d_low)  AS ceil_low,
       lo.mode * (pd.d_mode / cd.d_mode) AS ceil_mode,
       lo.high * (pd.d_high / cd.d_high) AS ceil_high,
       up.low, up.mode, up.high
FROM      (
    :compose(entries/couplings.sqlc)
) up
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) pf
       ON pf.composition = up.filing AND pf.composed_layer = up.from_layer
JOIN      (
    :compose(entries/couplings.sqlc)
) lo
       ON lo.filing = pf.part_filing AND lo.from_layer = pf.part_layer
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) pt
       ON pt.composition = up.filing AND pt.composed_layer = up.to_layer
      AND pt.part_layer = lo.to_layer
JOIN      (
    :compose(layers/demand.sqlc)
) pd
       ON pd.filing = pf.part_filing AND pd.layer = pf.part_layer
JOIN      (
    :compose(layers/demand.sqlc)
) cd
       ON cd.filing = up.filing      AND cd.layer = up.from_layer
WHERE up.mode IS NOT NULL AND lo.mode IS NOT NULL
